  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / User Execution Staging", "technique": "T1204.002", "what_happened": "At 2027-12-31 09:14:02 CET, Sysmon Event 11 fired on WS-HR-15 (10.30.6.78) for user CORP\\sara.k: a ZIP archive (presumably from a phishing email) was extracted to C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\, dropping two files in quick succession into the same user-writable directory. First file: targetFilename=C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\invoice_viewer.exe — signed by 'Microsoft Corporation' (Authenticode valid, signature chain OK). This is a LEGITIMATE Microsoft binary (a renamed copy of a sideloading-vulnerable signed app — Windows-bundled or known-vulnerable third-party) being smuggled by the attacker for use as a side-loading host. The signed status will help it bypass AV/EDR signature checks at execution.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 FileCreate on WS-HR-15: targetFilename ends in invoice_viewer.exe inside C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\, user=CORP\\sara.k. The benign-Microsoft-signed-status is exactly what makes side-loading attacks attractive — defenders trust signed binaries.", "first_seen": "2027-12-31T09:14:02+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Hijack Execution Flow Staging", "technique": "T1574.002", "what_happened": "At 09:14:02 CET (same second as step 1), Sysmon Event 11 fired again on WS-HR-15: targetFilename=C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\version.dll, signed=false, hash=e2c8d1a9b3f7c6d5a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1. The DLL named 'version.dll' is a well-known DLL search-order-hijack target — Microsoft Windows' standard version.dll (Version Information API) is a frequent side-loading target because many signed binaries call its exported functions (GetFileVersionInfoW, VerQueryValueW) without specifying a fully-qualified path. By placing a malicious version.dll in the SAME DIRECTORY as the legit signed exe, the attacker exploits the default DLL search order: the application directory is searched BEFORE %SYSTEMROOT%\\System32\\version.dll = the malicious DLL wins. This is MITRE T1574.002 DLL Side-Loading.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 FileCreate on WS-HR-15: targetFilename ends in version.dll in the SAME directory as the signed invoice_viewer.exe + signed=false + same user (CORP\\sara.k) + same second. The pairing of legit-signed-exe + unsigned-DLL-with-Windows-API-name in the same user-writable directory is the canonical side-loading staging signature.", "first_seen": "2027-12-31T09:14:02+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence / Scheduled Task Creation (Delayed Execution)", "technique": "T1053.005", "what_happened": "At 09:15:18 CET (76 seconds after drop), Sysmon Event 1 captured: image=C:\\Windows\\System32\\schtasks.exe, commandLine='schtasks.exe /create /tn \"InvoiceCheck\" /tr \"C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\invoice_viewer.exe\" /sc once /st 10:02 /f', parentImage=cmd.exe, user=CORP\\sara.k. A scheduled task named 'InvoiceCheck' was created targeting the dropped exe, scheduled to fire at 10:02 — approximately 47 minutes after the drop. The deliberate 48-minute delay between drop and execution is a TIME-BASED EVASION technique: behavioral EDR / sandbox solutions correlate file-drop with rapid execution; introducing a delay larger than typical detonation windows (5-10 min sandbox, 15-30 min on-host behavioral correlation) defeats those correlation rules. The task name 'InvoiceCheck' is masquerading-friendly (looks business-related).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=schtasks.exe + commandLine contains '/create' + '/tn \"InvoiceCheck\"' + '/tr' pointing to the user-directory exe + '/sc once' + '/st 10:02'. The 48-minute delay (drop at 09:14, execution scheduled for 10:02) is the diagnostic time-evasion signature.", "first_seen": "2027-12-31T09:15:18+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Execution / Side-Loaded Code", "technique": "T1574.002 + T1053.005", "what_happened": "At 10:02:41 CET (exactly when the scheduled task was set), Sysmon Event 1 fired: image=C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\invoice_viewer.exe, parentImage='Task Scheduler' (taskeng.exe / svchost.exe with task host context), user=CORP\\sara.k. The scheduled task triggered after the 48-minute delay — Task-Scheduler-as-parent for an EXE running from C:\\Users\\ is by itself a moderately suspicious pattern; combined with the staged DLL pair from steps 1-2, it's a clear side-loading detonation.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image points to the dropped exe + parentImage is Task Scheduler (taskeng/svchost task host) = the delayed scheduled-task execution fires. The image-path (C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\) is OUTSIDE the legit Microsoft install path for whatever signed app invoice_viewer.exe was renamed from = clear copy-out-of-install-path indicator.", "first_seen": "2027-12-31T10:02:41+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Hijack Execution Flow (Live Side-Load)", "technique": "T1574.002", "what_happened": "At 10:02:41 CET (same second as step 4 — the side-loading mechanic), Sysmon Event 7 ImageLoad fired: SourceImage=C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\invoice_viewer.exe, ImageLoaded=C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\version.dll, Signed=false, SignatureStatus=Unavailable, hash=e2c8d1a9b3f7c6d5a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1. THIS IS THE SMOKING GUN: a Microsoft-SIGNED binary (invoice_viewer.exe) loaded an UNSIGNED DLL (version.dll) from the SAME user-writable directory (C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\). For a Microsoft-signed binary, version.dll should ALWAYS load from C:\\Windows\\System32\\version.dll (signed by Microsoft, SHA hash known). The load of an unsigned version.dll from a user directory is impossible in a legitimate scenario — it can only happen when an attacker has placed a malicious DLL adjacent to a sideloading-vulnerable signed exe. The DLL is now in-process, executing within the trust context of the signed binary.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 7 ImageLoad: SourceImage is signed-Microsoft + ImageLoaded path is under C:\\Users\\ + ImageLoaded Signed=false + SignatureStatus=Unavailable. The combination IS the side-loading detection signature; no benign scenario produces this exact tuple.", "first_seen": "2027-12-31T10:02:41+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Command & Control (C2 Callback)", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 10:02:48 CET (7 seconds after the side-load), Sysmon Event 3 NetworkConnect: image=C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\invoice_viewer.exe, user=CORP\\sara.k, DestinationIp=185.220.101.42 (Hetzner NL — external), DestinationPort=443. The C2 callback fires from INSIDE the hijacked process — externally indistinguishable from a legitimate signed-binary HTTPS connection (Microsoft-signed process talking out on 443 = the kind of traffic egress filters typically allow), but in reality it's the attacker DLL using the host process's network privileges. Classic side-loading-for-evasion pattern.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: image=invoice_viewer.exe (the side-loading host) + DestinationIp=185.220.101.42 (external, Hetzner) + DestinationPort=443. The connection originates from a process that started executing seconds ago via scheduled task — no user-driven workflow explains the immediate outbound HTTPS.", "first_seen": "2027-12-31T10:02:48+0100"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Discovery / System Owner & Privilege Enumeration", "technique": "T1033 + T1059.003", "what_happened": "At 10:04:08 CET (87 seconds after C2 callback), Sysmon Event 1: image=C:\\Windows\\System32\\whoami.exe, commandLine='whoami /priv', parentImage=C:\\Users\\sara.k\\Documents\\invoice\\invoice_viewer.exe, user=CORP\\sara.k. The shellcode hosted by version.dll inside invoice_viewer.exe spawned a recon child — checking the user privileges available in the current token. parentImage being invoice_viewer.exe (not cmd.exe / explorer.exe) is the diagnostic detail: a Microsoft-signed binary is not supposed to be spawning whoami unless something is wrong with it. The post-C2 enumeration pattern confirms the implant is active and operator-controlled.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=whoami.exe + commandLine='whoami /priv' + parentImage=invoice_viewer.exe (NOT cmd / explorer / powershell). The signed-binary-as-parent-of-whoami pattern is a recognized side-loaded-implant signature.", "first_seen": "2027-12-31T10:04:08+0100"}
  ],
